{"reference":"2 Thessalonians 2","book":"2 Thessalonians","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter begins by addressing alarm that the day of the Lord had already arrived. It treats Jesus’ coming and the believers’ gathering to him as a coordinated subject, then contrasts the troubling claims with the lawless one’s defeat at Jesus’ appearing. The closing thanksgiving joins God’s choosing, salvation, sanctification by the Spirit, belief in truth, gospel calling, and sharing in Jesus’ glory.","verse_refs":["2 Thessalonians 2:1","2 Thessalonians 2:13","2 Thessalonians 2:13-14","2 Thessalonians 2:2","2 Thessalonians 2:8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_thessalonians_002_594196bd8dcf6e11235da739"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions in the Passage","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Several terms in the chapter are deliberately difficult to identify with precision. “Apostasy” most directly means rebellion or defection, but the text does not settle what specific rebellion is meant. Likewise, it speaks both of something that restrains and of one who restrains, without identifying either for later readers. Early textual witnesses also vary between “man of lawlessness” and “man of sin.” These uncertainties mean the passage’s portrayal is clear in broad outline—an opponent is revealed, exalts himself, deceives, and is finally defeated—while several details remain disputed.","verse_refs":["2 Thessalonians 2:1-17","2 Thessalonians 2:3","2 Thessalonians 2:3-12","2 Thessalonians 2:6-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_thessalonians_002_8e71cdad1131b3af7bb4767e"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The lawless one’s activity is described with Satanic power, signs, false wonders, and unrighteous deception. His self-exaltation includes sitting in “the temple of God,” but the passage does not determine whether that temple should be understood as earthly, heavenly, communal, or symbolic. In contrast to messages that caused alarm, the readers are told to hold to the traditions taught through spoken instruction or letter.","verse_refs":["2 Thessalonians 2:15","2 Thessalonians 2:4","2 Thessalonians 2:9-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_thessalonians_002_9f21c8b39a32ba5e7d9b7b57"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}